–coloring, therefore\\nχ(C2k+1 ⊕C2n+1) ≤7. As the lower bound follows from Theorem 2, the\\nproof is complete. 2\\nWe conclude the paper by showing that some colorings give us 3–regular\\n3–uniform hypergraphs.\\nProposition 11 Let G and H be graphs isomorphic to C7. Let f be a 7–\\ncoloring of G⊕H. Let FG = {f(Gx) | x ∈V (H)} and let FH = {f(Ha) | a ∈\\nV (G)}. Then (IN7, FG) and (IN7, FH) are 3–regular 3–uniform hypergraphs.\\nProof. It is enough to prove the proposition for (IN7, FG). Let V (H) =\\n{v1, v2, . . . , v7}. We claim that |f(Gvi)| = 3, i ∈{1, 2, . . . , 7}. Clearly,\\n|f(Gvi)| ≤4, for otherwise |f(Gvi) ∪f(Gvi+1)| ≥8.\\nSuppose now that\\n|f(Gv1)| = 4. Then f(Gv2) = f(Gv7) and |f(Gv2)| = 3. Hence f(Gv3) ⊆\\nf(Gv1) and f(Gv6) ⊆f(Gv1). Therefore, |f(Gv3) ∪f(Gv6)| ≥2. But then\\nthere are at most 5 colors left to color the layers Gv4 and Gv5. This contra-\\ndiction proves the claim. It follows that (IN7, FG) is 3–uniform. Furthermore,\\nas |FG| = |IN7| = 7 we conclude that (IN7, FG) is a 3–regular hypergraph. 2\\nReferences\\n[1] M. Borowiecki, On chromatic number of products of two graphs, Col-\\nloquium Math. 25 (1972) 49–52.\\n[2] J. Feigenbaum and A.A. Sch¨aﬀer, Finding the prime factors of strong\\ndirect product graphs in polynomial time, Discrete Math. 109 (1992)\\n77–102.\\n[3] D. Geller and S. Stahl, The chromatic number and other parameters of\\nthe lexicographic product, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 19 (1975) 87–95.\\n[4] P. Hell and F. Roberts, Analogues of the Shannon capacity of a graph,\\nAnnals of Discrete Math. 12 (1982) 155–168.\\n[5] S. Klavˇzar, Two remarks on retracts of graph products, Discrete Math.\\n109 (1992) 155–160.\\n[6] S. Klavˇzar, Strong products of χ-critical graphs, Aequationes Math., in\\npress.\\n9--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTAIWANESE JOURNAL OF MATHEMATICS (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=2bd22aa4e9e356702a2eaf6b093d7827d420c7c4&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\\ncite