le graph $G$ in answer to the stated problem.\\nTitle: On Chromatic Core Subgraph of Simple Graphs\\nAuthors: Johan Kok, Sudev Naduvath, M. Jerlin Seles, U. Mary\\nDate: Mon Mar  5 05:48:00 2018\\n\\nIf distinct colours represent distinct technology types that are placed at the vertices of a simple graph in accordance to a minimum proper colouring, a disaster recovery strategy could rely on an answer to the question: \\\"What is the maximum destruction, if any, the graph (a network) can undergo while ensuring that at least one of each technology type remain, in accordance to a minimum proper colouring of the remaining induced subgraph.\\\" In this paper, we introduce the notion of a chromatic core subgraph $H$ of a given simple graph $G$ in answer to the stated problem. Since for any subgraph $H$ of $G$ it holds that $\\\\chi(H) \\\\leq \\\\chi(G)$, the problem is well defined.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nOn the chromatic number of the lexicographic product and the Cartesian sum of graphs (https://www.academia.edu/164692418/On_the_chromatic_number_of_the_lexicographic_product_and_the_Cartesian_sum_of_graphs)\\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; In the next section we prove a lower bound for the chromatic number of the lexicographic product of graphs: if G is a nonbipartite graph, then for any graph H, χ(G[H]) ≥ 2χ(H) + d χ(H) k e, where 2k + 1 is the length of a shortest odd cycle of G.\\n Let ∗ be a graph product. For x ∈ V (H) set Gx = G ∗ {x}. Analogously we define Ha for a ∈ V (G). We call Gx and Ha a layer of G and H, respectively. Let f be a coloring of G ∗ H. The set of all colors with respect to f in a layer Gx will be briefly denoted by f (Gx ). In the next section we prove a lower bound for the chromatic number of the lexicographic product of graphs: if G is a nonbipartite graph, then for any graph H, χ(G[H]) ≥ 2χ(H) + d χ(H) k e, where 2k + 1 is the length of a shortest odd cycle of G. In Section 3 we consider chromatic numbers of the Cartesian sum of graphs. We prove that for nontrivial χ–critical graphs G and H, χ(G ⊕ H) ≤ χ(G)χ(H) − 1, thus generalizing two results from [10, 12]. In the last section we apply these bounds to show that for n ≥ k ≥ 2, χ(C2k+1 ⊕ C2n+1 ) = χ(C2k+1 [C2n+1 ]) = ( 8; k = 2, 7; k ≥ 3. We finally give a connection of some colorings with hypergraphs. 2 A lower bound for the lexicographic product Chromatic numbers of the lexicographic product have been investigated in [3, 4, 6, 7, 11]. Geller and Stahl [3] proved that if G has at least one edge, then χ(G[H]) ≥ χ(G) + 2χ(H) − 2 for any graph H. This bound is the best general lower bound known so far. A short proof of the bound is given in [7]. The main theorem of this section (Theorem 2) implies another lower bound and is essentially proved in [11, Theorem 6]. However, our 2 proof is straightforward and simple. For the proof we need the following: Lemma 1 Let X, A and B be any (finite) sets. Then |A ∩ B| + |X| ≥ |A ∩ X| + |B ∩ X|. Theorem 2 For any graph H and any k ≥ 1, χ(C2k+1 [H]) = 2χ(H) + d χ(H) k e. Proof. We first prove the lower bound: χ(C2k+1 [H]) ≥ 2χ(H) + d χ(H) k e. Let χ(H) = n. It is easy to verify that the bound holds for k = 1 and for k ≥ n.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAcyclic coloring of products of digraphs (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X24000283)\\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Regarding the lexicographic product, the following has been proved in [7]. In Section 3, we show that the analogous holds for the dichromatic number.\\n\\nTheorem 4 Geller, Stahl [7] ... More recently, Pleanmani and Panma [14] proved that this bound is tight when G$G$ is the directed cycle on n$n$ vertices and H$H$ is such that n>{\\\\chi }_{a}\\\\left(H\\\\right)$n>{\\\\chi }_{a}\\\\left(H\\\\right)$.\\n--------------------------------